.TH LD.SO 8 2026-10-03 "Substrate" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
ld.so, ld64.so \- Substrate dynamic linker/loader
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /sbin/ld.so
.br
.I /sbin/ld64.so
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B /sbin/ld.so
loads the shared libraries a dynamically linked Substrate program
needs, relocates them and the program, runs their constructors and
then jumps to the program's entry point.
It is not run by hand: the kernel maps it as the program interpreter
of any i386 executable whose
.B PT_INTERP
names
.IR /sbin/ld.so ,
and passes it the program's headers through the auxiliary vector.
.PP
.B ld.so
is freestanding.
It links against no library and performs its own system calls.
.PP
.B /sbin/ld64.so
is the same linker built for amd64.
It is the interpreter of 64-bit executables, which run on the x86_64
kernel only, and loads 64-bit libraries from its own directories.
This page describes the 32-bit linker; where the 64-bit one differs,
see
.BR "The 64-bit linker" .
.SS Startup
.IP 1. 4
.B ld.so
applies its own
.B R_386_RELATIVE
relocations, then locates the program's
.BR PT_DYNAMIC .
A program without
.B PT_DYNAMIC
(a static PIE) is entered directly.
.IP 2.
Objects named by
.B LD_PRELOAD
are loaded (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ).
.IP 3.
The
.B DT_NEEDED
entries of the program, and then of every loaded object, are loaded
breadth-first.
The resulting list, program first, is the global symbol lookup scope.
A dependency that cannot be found is fatal.
.IP 4.
Thread-local storage is laid out (see
.BR "Thread-local storage" ).
.IP 5.
Every object is relocated, with
.B R_386_COPY
relocations applied last so they copy already-relocated data.
Binding is always immediate; there is no lazy PLT binding.
.IP 6.
Each library's
.B PT_LOAD
segments are returned to their ELF protections (they are mapped
writable for relocation) and each
.B PT_GNU_RELRO
region is made read-only.
.IP 7.
.B environ
is set, if some object defines it, so constructors may call
.BR getenv (3).
.IP 8.
.B DT_INIT
and then
.B DT_INIT_ARRAY
run for every object, deepest dependency first and the program last.
.PP
When the program calls
.BR exit (3),
libc calls back into
.B ld.so
to run each object's
.B DT_FINI_ARRAY
(in reverse order) and then
.BR DT_FINI ,
program first.
.SS Library search
A
.B DT_NEEDED
name, an
.B LD_PRELOAD
entry, or a
.BR dlopen (3)
argument is resolved as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
If an object with that name (its
.BR DT_SONAME ,
or its file name if it has none) is already loaded, it is reused.
.IP \(bu
A name beginning with
.B /
is opened as given.
.IP \(bu
Any other name is appended to each of
.IR /lib ,
.I /usr/lib
and
.IR /usr/local/lib ,
in that order, and then to each directory listed in
.IR /etc/ld.so.conf .
The first file that loads is used.
.PP
.B DT_RPATH
and
.B DT_RUNPATH
are ignored, and
.B LD_LIBRARY_PATH
is not supported; a library outside the built-in directories must be in
a directory named by
.IR /etc/ld.so.conf .
.PP
After a file is mapped, its
.B DT_SONAME
is compared with the objects already loaded; if it matches one, the new
mapping is discarded and the existing object is used.
.PP
A file is accepted only if it is an
.B ELFCLASS32
.B ET_DYN
object for
.B EM_386
with at most 64 program headers and a
.BR PT_DYNAMIC
segment.
A file that is not is skipped, and the search continues with the next
directory.
The
.B EI_OSABI
byte is not checked.
.SS /etc/ld.so.conf
The file is read once, the first time a name is not found in the
built-in directories.
Each line holds one absolute directory.
Leading and trailing blanks are ignored, as are empty lines and lines
beginning with
.BR # .
A line of the form
.PP
.RS
.BI include " pattern"
.RE
.PP
reads further configuration files.
.I pattern
is either a file name or a path whose final component contains
.B *
or
.B ?
wildcards, which are matched against the entries of that directory.
Includes may nest four deep.
At most 64 distinct directories and 8 KiB of configuration text in
total are used; anything beyond is ignored.
A missing file adds no directories.
.SS Symbol resolution
Symbols are looked up in each object of the global scope in load
order, using
.B DT_GNU_HASH
when present and
.B DT_HASH
otherwise.
The first global or weak definition wins.
GNU symbol versioning
.RB ( DT_VERSYM ,
.BR DT_VERDEF ,
.BR DT_VERNEED )
is honoured: a versioned reference binds only to a definition of that
version, and an unversioned reference does not bind to a hidden
non-default version.
An undefined weak reference is left as 0; any other undefined symbol
is fatal.
.PP
When a non-PIE executable takes the address of a shared-library
function, the executable's PLT entry is used as that function's
address by every other object, so function pointers compare equal
across objects.
.PP
Supported relocation types are
.BR R_386_NONE ,
.BR R_386_32 ,
.BR R_386_PC32 ,
.BR R_386_COPY ,
.BR R_386_GLOB_DAT ,
.BR R_386_JMP_SLOT ,
.BR R_386_RELATIVE ,
.BR R_386_TLS_TPOFF ,
.B R_386_TLS_DTPMOD32
and
.BR R_386_TLS_DTPOFF32 .
Any other type is fatal.
.SS Thread-local storage
.B ld.so
uses the i386 variant II layout: each module's
.B PT_TLS
block sits below the thread pointer, which points at a two-word thread
control block (a self-pointer at
.B %gs:0
and the dynamic thread vector at
.BR %gs:4 ).
At startup every
.B PT_TLS
module gets a fixed offset, a further 4096 bytes (and 16 module slots)
are reserved as surplus for modules loaded later with
.BR dlopen (3),
and the block is installed as the initial thread's
.B %gs
base.
The total per-thread block is limited to 32 KiB.
libpthread obtains a block of the same layout for each new thread from
.BR ld.so .
.PP
When
.BR dlopen (3)
loads a module with
.BR PT_TLS ,
it is placed in the surplus and its initialization image is copied
into every live thread, so initial-exec references work immediately.
The general-dynamic model is served by
.B __tls_get_addr
(also provided by libc), which fills in a thread's slot on first use.
.SS Run-time interface
.B ld.so
exports the functions behind libdl's
.BR dlopen (3),
.BR dlsym ,
.BR dlclose ,
.BR dlerror ,
.B dladdr
and
.BR dl_iterate_phdr ;
programs call them through libdl, not directly.
.IP \(bu 2
.B dlopen
loads the object and its
.B DT_NEEDED
closure, relocates and protects them and runs their constructors.
If any step fails every object loaded by that call is unmapped.
.BR RTLD_LAZY ,
.BR RTLD_NOW ,
.B RTLD_GLOBAL
and
.B RTLD_LOCAL
are ignored: binding is immediate and every object joins the global
scope.
.B RTLD_NOLOAD
returns a handle only if the object is already loaded.
.B dlopen(NULL)
returns a handle for the program.
.IP \(bu
.B dlsym
with
.B RTLD_DEFAULT
or the program's handle searches the global scope;
with
.B RTLD_NEXT
it searches the objects after the caller's; with any other handle it
searches only that object.
.IP \(bu
.B dlclose
decrements a reference count and, when it reaches zero and no loaded
object lists the library in
.BR DT_NEEDED ,
runs its destructors.
The object is never unmapped.
.IP \(bu
.B dlerror
returns the most recent error message once.
The message is shared by all threads.
.PP
The
.B dl*
calls are serialized by one process-wide recursive lock, so a
constructor may itself call
.BR dlopen .
.SS The 64-bit linker
.B /sbin/ld64.so
is built from the same sources and behaves as described above, with
these differences:
.IP \(bu 2
It is the interpreter of
.B ELFCLASS64
executables whose
.B PT_INTERP
names
.IR /sbin/ld64.so ,
and accepts only
.B ELFCLASS64
objects for
.BR EM_X86_64 .
.IP \(bu
Its built-in directories are
.IR /lib64 ,
.I /usr/lib64
and
.IR /usr/local/lib64 .
It reads the same
.I /etc/ld.so.conf
as the 32-bit linker; a directory listed there that holds only 32-bit
libraries contributes nothing, because objects of the wrong class are
skipped.
.IP \(bu
Relocations are read from
.B DT_RELA
tables.
The supported types are
.BR R_X86_64_NONE ,
.BR R_X86_64_64 ,
.BR R_X86_64_PC32 ,
.BR R_X86_64_COPY ,
.BR R_X86_64_GLOB_DAT ,
.BR R_X86_64_JUMP_SLOT ,
.BR R_X86_64_RELATIVE ,
.BR R_X86_64_TPOFF64 ,
.B R_X86_64_DTPMOD64
and
.BR R_X86_64_DTPOFF64 .
.B R_X86_64_IRELATIVE
(indirect functions) is not supported and, like any other type, is
fatal.
.IP \(bu
The thread pointer is the
.B %fs
base.
The thread control block is 64 bytes, with the self-pointer at
.B %fs:0
and the dynamic thread vector at
.BR %fs:8 ,
and the argument of
.B __tls_get_addr
is two 8-byte words.
.IP \(bu
It names itself
.B ld64.so
in the
.B LD_TRACE_LOADED_OBJECTS
listing and in its fatal-error message, and prints addresses as 16
hexadecimal digits.
.SH EXIT STATUS
On a fatal error (a missing dependency, an undefined symbol, an
unsupported relocation, or a TLS setup failure)
.B ld.so
prints a diagnostic and exits with status 127 before the program runs.
With
.B LD_TRACE_LOADED_OBJECTS
set it exits with status 0 after printing the object list.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
All of the following are ignored when the kernel marks the execution
as secure
.RB ( AT_SECURE ,
set for set-user-ID and set-group-ID programs).
.TP
.B LD_PRELOAD
A list of libraries, separated by colons or spaces, to load after the
program and before its
.B DT_NEEDED
libraries, so that their definitions take precedence over those of the
program's libraries (but not of the program itself).
Each entry is resolved as described under
.BR "Library search" .
An entry that cannot be loaded is reported and skipped.
.TP
.B LD_TRACE_LOADED_OBJECTS
If set, to any value including the empty string,
.B ld.so
loads and relocates everything as usual, then prints one line per
loaded object other than the program and exits instead of running the
program.
Each line has the form
.RS
.IP
.EX
<tab>\fIname\fP => \fIpath\fP (\fIbase\fP)
.EE
.RE
.IP
where
.I name
is the object's
.B DT_SONAME
and
.I base
its load bias in hexadecimal.
.B ld.so
itself is listed as
.IR "ld.so (0x...)" .
This is what
.BR ldd (1)
uses.
.TP
.B LD_DEBUG
If set, to any value, print a trace of the auxiliary vector, loaded
objects, relocation passes, TLS layout and constructor calls to
standard error.
The value is not examined.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /sbin/ld.so
The dynamic linker for 32-bit programs.
.TP
.I /sbin/ld64.so
The dynamic linker for 64-bit programs.
.TP
.IR /lib ", " /usr/lib ", " /usr/local/lib
Built-in library directories of
.BR ld.so ,
searched first.
.TP
.IR /lib64 ", " /usr/lib64 ", " /usr/local/lib64
Built-in library directories of
.BR ld64.so .
.TP
.I /etc/ld.so.conf
Additional library directories.
.TP
.I /etc/ld.so.conf.d/*.conf
Drop-in files included by the stock
.IR /etc/ld.so.conf .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ld (1),
.BR ldd (1),
.BR brandelf (1),
.BR readelf (1),
.BR execve (2),
.BR dlopen (3),
.BR libsys (7)
.SH BUGS
All diagnostics, and the
.B LD_TRACE_LOADED_OBJECTS
listing, are written to standard error, not standard output.
.PP
An
.B LD_PRELOAD
entry such as
.I ./libfoo.so
that contains a slash but does not start with one is searched for in
the library directories rather than opened relative to the current
directory.
.PP
At most 192 objects can be loaded, and library paths are limited to
255 bytes.
.PP
There is no lazy binding, no
.BR LD_LIBRARY_PATH ,
no
.BR DT_RPATH / DT_RUNPATH
support, no
.B RTLD_LOCAL
scope, and
.BR dlclose (3)
never unmaps anything.
A dlopened module whose TLS needs more than the remaining surplus
cannot be loaded.
